1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,000
One sky. The Tukano saw — a whole year
of floods.

2
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:09,000
Northwest Brazil, the Tiquié River: for
the Tukano, the most important meat is
fish.

3
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:15,000
What matters is not when a constellation
rises, but when it sets.

4
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:20,000
After sunset they look west: which one
is sinking now?

5
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:32,000
So floods are named for constellations:
pamõ poero is “the armadillo’s flood.”

6
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:36,000
February: a little fish, and the shrimp
chasing it.

7
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:48,000
Late April: Ñohkoatero, “a group of
stars” — the Pleiades. The Tukano here
say the new year begins.

8
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:55,000
September to November: Aña, from
Scorpius to Sagittarius. Red Antares is
its venom sac.

9
00:00:55,000 --> 00:01:02,000
December: the armadillo. It is also a
sacred flute; only men know its true
shape.

10
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:17,000
After dark, look low in the northwest:
the west half of Cassiopeia’s W is the
jaguar’s head.

11
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:23,000
On the Tiquié it is touching the
horizon: by their calendar, the jaguar’s
flood.

12
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:26,000
Tupi-Guarani: a sky written down in
1614.

13
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:29,000
Follow for a new sky every week.

